# Cloudflare Pages security headers — applied to every response.
# Vite copies this file from public/ into dist/ on build.
#
# The CSP is ENFORCING. It was verified in Report-Only mode first (clean console
# on a full run: game, Wikipedia card, leaderboard, Turnstile). If you ever need
# to debug a new violation without blocking users, temporarily rename
# `Content-Security-Policy:` to `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only:`.
#
# The CSP allowlist covers the only external origins the app actually uses:
#   • Supabase          — auth + RPC/REST            (connect-src *.supabase.co)
#   • Cloudflare Turnstile — captcha script + iframe (script/frame/connect challenges.cloudflare.com)
#   • Wikipedia         — REST summary API           (connect-src en.wikipedia.org)
#                         + lead images              (img-src *.wikimedia.org, *.wikipedia.org)
#     WILDCARDED on purpose. It was upload.wikimedia.org until Wikipedia began serving
#     summary thumbnails from thumb.wikimedia.org, which the policy did not allow, so those
#     tiles rendered as broken images with nothing in the app able to detect it: the URL is
#     valid and returns 200, the browser simply refuses to load it. Roughly one species in ten
#     was affected. Naming hosts one at a time means the next host they add breaks tiles again
#     the same silent way, and every wikimedia.org subdomain serves the same media anyway.
#   • iNaturalist       — species photographs        (img-src inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com)
#     No connect-src entry, and that is not an oversight: the photo map is baked at build
#     time (scripts/pull-inat-photos.mjs), so the app never calls their API. It only loads
#     <img> URLs it already knows. ONLY the open-data bucket is listed, which is also the
#     licence boundary — iNaturalist serves CC-licensed photos from there and
#     all-rights-reserved ones from static.inaturalist.org, so a photo we may not use
#     cannot load even if a bad URL ever reached the client.
# The three inline <script>s in index.html (legacy-domain redirect, theme-before-
# paint, then the stale-asset reload guard) are each allowed by their own sha256
# hash, NOT 'unsafe-inline', so injected scripts stay blocked. If any snippet is
# edited, recompute its hash or it will be blocked — and a blocked reload guard
# means a blank page after a deploy, which is exactly what it exists to prevent:
#   python3 - <<'PY'
#   import re, hashlib, base64
#   html = open('index.html', encoding='utf-8').read()
#   for m in re.finditer(r'<script(?![^>]*\bsrc=)[^>]*>(.*?)</script>', html, re.S):
#       print(base64.b64encode(hashlib.sha256(m.group(1).encode()).digest()).decode())
#   PY
# 'unsafe-inline' in style-src is required for React inline style={} attributes.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-PO/pQhqitWhIIx4ClFM7VI1eOVPp/jW60mWSPv7RffI=' 'sha256-pREEHP1tF4hnFjsGWhCxTHPIc2H7oIFfztRnZeIy1s8=' 'sha256-gDzmt7a9vTUvBF/8qnm5VK4YUu1Hk+4a3O7/DekdwOs=' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: https://*.wikimedia.org https://*.wikipedia.org https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co https://en.wikipedia.org https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# Cache strategy (SPA). index.html is the entry point and names the hashed JS/CSS
# bundles; it MUST always be revalidated, or a returning browser keeps an old
# index.html that points at a bundle a later deploy has removed. 'no-cache' means
# "store, but revalidate every time" (cheap 304s), so a new deploy is picked up
# immediately.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Build identity, rewritten by every deploy and polled by open tabs so they can
# reload themselves onto a new version (src/data/versionCheck.ts). A cached copy
# would mean a tab never sees the change, which is the whole point of the file.
/version.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Hashed build assets. The filename carries a content hash, so a URL's bytes never
# change and it would be safe to cache for a year — but a header rule matches a
# PATH, not a response, so an error served under /assets/* inherits whatever is set
# here. On 2026-08-04 a stylesheet request lost a race with a deploy, Pages answered
# with index.html, and `immutable, max-age=31536000` pinned that wrong response to a
# valid URL at Cloudflare's edge, for every visitor, with no way to revalidate. A
# day is the whole point of the shorter window: any such entry heals itself, and
# dropping `immutable` gives users an explicit reload as an escape hatch. It costs a
# conditional request per asset per day (a 304, no re-download), which is nothing
# next to a deploy that changes the URLs anyway.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
